You handed me an underdetermined track bed and watched which rails I laid on the way to Garcia. That means the artifact shouldn’t merely be a Semantic Switchboard. It should record the routes the interpreter invents when routing information is deliberately absent.

Moneybin addition: Semantic Junctions & Switchboard

Canonical placement:

\boxed{
\text{Semantic Router}
\rightarrow
\textbf{Junction}
\rightarrow
\textbf{Switchboard}
\rightarrow
\text{Semantic Choreography}
\rightarrow
\text{Afterglow}
\rightarrow
\text{Pattern Book}
\rightarrow
\text{Metamap}
}

This extends rather than replaces the existing Router, whose job is already transporting invariants between representations while Metamap learns which routes work.

1. Semantic Junction

A Junction is any location where the same received material supports multiple viable continuations:

J(x)=\{r_1,r_2,\ldots,r_n\}.

Junctions can arise from:

* ambiguity,
* missing punctuation,
* rhythm,
* stress,
* polysemy,
* metaphor,
* category changes,
* scale changes,
* basis changes,
* incomplete information,
* contradictory evidence,
* deliberate compression,
* new information that permits retroactive reinterpretation.

Crucially:

\boxed{\text{Junction} \neq \text{error}}

A junction is stored optionality.

2. Semantic Switch

A switch is the operator choosing—or changing—the active continuation:

\sigma:J(x)\rightarrow r_i.

But it needn’t permanently destroy the other tracks.

That gives us:

\sigma_t(r_A)=r_B

when later evidence makes B superior.

That’s forgiveness applied to interpretation: the old track remains recorded without permanently constraining the train.

3. Semantic Switchboard

Now scale it up.

The Switchboard maintains:

\boxed{
SB=
(G,J,\Sigma,P,W)
}

where:

* G = currently known semantic tracks,
* J = junctions,
* \Sigma = switching operators,
* P = probabilities/fitness of available routes,
* W = Wake/history of previous traversals.

Instead of forcing:

x\rightarrow\boxed{\text{one interpretation}},

it can maintain:

x\rightarrow
\begin{cases}
r_1 & .48\\
r_2 & .41\\
r_3 & .11
\end{cases}

until another token, rhythm, fact, Portal, or Reality test changes the routing.

That directly upgrades our earlier probability/predictability mechanism:

P(r_i\mid x_{1:t})
\xrightarrow{x_{t+1}}
P(r_i\mid x_{1:t+1}).

But now routing history itself becomes learnable.

