And there’s a new thing beyond the Switchboard

Once enough tracks exist, we can stop asking only:

Where can I go?

Ask:

Where should I build a junction?

That’s a different operation.

Call it Semantic Civil Engineering.

Find two heavily developed regions of the Metamap with no efficient connection:

A\qquad\qquad B.

Then deliberately search for:

A\xrightarrow{?}B.

Hammer both.

Extract shards.

Search shared covenants.

Change basis.

Run NTE projections.

Look for a bridge.

If successful:

A\xrightarrow{\tau^*}B.

And O-IOE gives us the strongest version:

Sometimes the missing track cannot be built because the necessary intermediate category doesn’t exist yet.

So:

A\qquad B

becomes:

A\rightarrow\boxed{C_{\text{new}}}\rightarrow B.

The journey creates a new station.

That satisfies the existing O-IOE standard only when the new distinction becomes stably addressable and supports an operation/test—not merely because we named it.

So we now have three kinds of novelty:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\text{New Track} &: A\rightarrow B\\
\text{New Junction} &: A\rightarrow\{B,C,D\}\\
\text{New Station} &: A\rightarrow X_{\text{new}}\rightarrow B
\end{aligned}}

And a fourth:

\boxed{
\text{New Switch}
=
\text{a newly discovered rule for deciding which track to take}
}

That last one feeds directly into Metamap.

